{
  "id": "132-pattern",
  "questionId": "132-pattern",
  "slug": "132-pattern",
  "title": "132 Pattern",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Monotonic stack",
  "patterns": [
    "monotonic stack",
    "array"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "think of nums[k] as playing the role of '2' - it needs to be less than some '3' (nums[j]) but greater than some earlier '1' (nums[i]). if you scan from the right, which of these three roles becomes easiest to fix first?",
    "scan from right to left, treating the current element as a candidate '1'. you need to know: is there some pair further to the right where a bigger value came before a smaller-but-still-bigger-than-current value?",
    "maintain a monotonic decreasing stack of values (candidates for '3'), and a running variable for the best '2' found so far, initialized very small. whenever the current element is smaller than that '2' value, you've found your '1' and can return true",
    "whenever the current element is greater than the top of the stack, pop values off the stack, updating your '2' value to the largest one popped (since a smaller value popped later becomes a worse, i.e. less useful, '2' candidate), then push the current element"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using non-strict inequalities (<=) somewhere, which would allow equal values to incorrectly count as a valid pattern",
    "trying to fix i first and scan forward for j and k, which makes it hard to know the best possible '2' efficiently",
    "updating the '2' candidate to the wrong popped value (it should be the largest one popped, not the last one)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given an array of integers `nums`.\n\n**Determine whether there exist three indices `i`, `j`, `k` with `i < j < k` such that `nums[i] < nums[k] < nums[j]`.**\n\nThis shape (a small value, then a big value, then a middle value) is called a \"132 pattern\" because the three values' relative sizes look like 1, 3, 2 in that order.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length",
      "nums may contain negative numbers",
      "the three indices must satisfy i < j < k strictly"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "an array with fewer than 3 elements (never has a valid pattern)",
      "the pattern requires values from far apart, not just adjacent indices",
      "duplicate values, where strict inequality must still hold"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
